    <body>
      <sec id="article-22593.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Head and neck cancer is the sixth most common malignancy worldwide, with squamous cell carcinoma accounting for the majority of cases arising in the oral cavity and pharynx. Prognosis is strongly influenced by the status of the cervical lymph nodes, which represent the most important determinant of survival. The presence of cervical lymph node metastases reduces the 5-year survival rate by approximately 50% compared with patients without nodal involvement. According to the American Cancer Society, nearly 40% of patients with squamous cell carcinoma of the oral cavity and pharynx present with cervical lymph node metastases at diagnosis. Advances in oncologic therapy have improved survival; however, preservation of anatomical structures and functional outcomes has become a central priority in contemporary head and neck cancer management.</p>
        <p>This activity reviews the etiology and pathophysiology of head and neck cancer and outlines the indications and contraindications for surgical intervention. This activity also provides healthcare professionals with the knowledge required to support clinical decision-making and optimize postoperative outcomes, with emphasis on techniques that preserve anatomical integrity and functional status following treatment.&#x000a0;In addition, this activity emphasizes the importance of effective interprofessional collaboration among surgeons, oncologists, rehabilitation specialists, and nursing staff to ensure coordinated care, enhance recovery, and promote long-term functional outcomes in patients undergoing treatment for head and neck cancer.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the anatomical levels of cervical lymph nodes and their relevance to patterns of metastatic spread in head and neck cancer.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based surgical planning strategies that account for tumor site, nodal involvement, and reconstructive need.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select appropriate neck dissection techniques based on tumor histology, staging, and patient-specific factors.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with an interprofessional healthcare team, including oncology, radiology, pathology, rehabilitation, and nursing, to optimize patient care.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=22593&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=22593">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Head and neck&#x000a0;cancer&#x000a0;is the sixth most common cancer worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;The most important prognostic factor in squamous cell carcinoma is the status of the cervical lymph nodes. Surgical removal of cervical lymph nodes for oncologic purposes is termed a &#x0201c;neck dissection.&#x0201d; The procedure is tailored to address lymph node basins at risk for metastatic spread based on the primary tumor site. The presence of positive cervical lymph nodes reduces the 5-year survival rate by approximately 50% compared with patients who have negative cervical lymph nodes.</p>
        <p>The American Cancer Society reports that approximately 40% of patients with squamous cell carcinoma of the oral cavity and pharynx present with regional metastases to the cervical lymph nodes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r2">[2]</xref> Accordingly, management of the cervical lymph nodes is a critical component of the overall treatment strategy for&#x000a0;patients with squamous cell carcinoma of the head and neck. Surgical treatment of head and neck cancer is a major undertaking associated with significant potential morbidity; moreover, not all patients are optimal candidates for surgery with a high likelihood of favorable outcomes.</p>
        <p>Accordingly, appropriate patient selection is essential and should include thorough preoperative cardiac and pulmonary evaluation, along with accurate cancer staging. Although surgery provides superior local control, overall survival is often comparable to that achieved with nonsurgical treatments for many head and neck cancers. Survival outcomes continue to improve following head and neck treatment, resulting in longer posttreatment survival and increased relevance of treatment-related morbidity. Therefore, astute&#x000a0;clinicians must individualize treatment strategies to optimize quality of life and preserve posttreatment function.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s3" sec-type="Anatomy and Physiology">
        <title>Anatomy and Physiology</title>
        <p>Cervical lymph node classification is based on a system&#x000a0;developed at Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center in the 1930s.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;This system divides the lymph nodes in the lateral aspect of the neck into 5 nodal levels (I through V), as described below. The lymph nodes in the central compartment&#x000a0;are designated as level VI, and those in the superior anterior mediastinum as&#x000a0;level VII. The clinical and surgical landmarks for neck node levels are mentioned below.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Level I</bold> (submental triangle is level Ia; submandibular triangles are level Ib) borders are:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Superior: Lower border of the mandible</p></list-item><list-item><p>Posterior: Posterior belly of the digastric muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inferior: Hyoid bone</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Level II</bold> (upper jugular lymph nodes; level IIa begins at the posterior border of the submandibular gland and extends to the accessory nerve; level IIb consists of node-bearing tissue bordered anteriorly by the accessory nerve and posteriorly by the trapezius and suboccipital muscles) borders are:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Superior: Base of the skull</p></list-item><list-item><p>Posterior: Posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anterior: Lateral limit of the sternohyoid&#x000a0;muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inferior: Hyoid bone</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Level III</bold> (middle jugular lymph nodes) borders are:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Superior: Hyoid bone</p></list-item><list-item><p>Posterior: Posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid&#x000a0;muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anterior: Lateral limit of the sternohyoid&#x000a0;muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inferior: Cricothyroid membrane&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Level IV</bold> (lower jugular lymph nodes) borders are:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Superior: Cricothyroid membrane</p></list-item><list-item><p>Posterior: Posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid&#x000a0;muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anterior: Lateral limit of the sternohyoid&#x000a0;muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inferior: Clavicle&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Level V</bold> (posterior triangle of the neck: level Va is the tissue above the posterior belly of the omohyoid muscle; level Vb is the node-bearing tissue between the posterior belly of the omohyoid muscle and the clavicle/thoracic inlet) borders are:&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Posterior: Anterior border of the trapezius muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Anterior: Posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inferior: Clavicle&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Level VI</bold>&#x000a0;(central compartment of the neck) borders are:
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Superior: Hyoid bone</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inferior: Suprasternal notch</p></list-item><list-item><p>Lateral: Medial borders of the carotid sheath on either side</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Level VII</bold> (superior mediastinal lymph nodes) borders are:&#x000a0;
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Superior: Suprasternal notch</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inferior: Innominate artery</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s4" sec-type="Indications">
        <title>Indications</title>
        <p>Before discussing the indications for neck dissection and the operative technique, it is essential to review the brief background, history, and types of neck dissection. The credit for neck dissection as a curative procedure for cervical metastases belongs to George Washington Crile from the Cleveland Clinic.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;In 1900, he performed various forms of neck dissection and subsequently described the classic radical neck dissection (RND) in his seminal 1905 publication in the&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Transactions of the Southern Surgical and Gynecological Association.</italic>&#x000a0;This operation serves as the basis for all subsequent neck dissection techniques, with later surgeries framed as modifications of this initial operation.</p>
        <p>Hayes Martin of&#x000a0;Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center&#x000a0;popularized the radical neck dissection by describing its stepwise technique in a landmark 1951 publication. However, this operative procedure is associated with significant morbidity, including cosmetic deformity and impaired shoulder function resulting from en bloc resection of the spinal accessory nerve, sternocleidomastoid muscle, internal jugular vein, and the tail of the parotid gland.</p>
        <p>Argentinean surgeon Oswaldo Suarez was the first to describe functional neck dissection in 1963, now known as modified RND. This approach involves the removal of all 5 cervical lymph node levels while preserving the spinal accessory nerve, sternocleidomastoid muscle, and internal jugular vein, thereby reducing the shoulder.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The types and indications of neck dissection are&#x000a0;as follows:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Radical neck dissection: This procedure involves the removal of lymph nodes from levels I to V, along with the ipsilateral sternocleidomastoid muscle, internal jugular vein, and spinal accessory nerve. The tail of the parotid gland is rarely included in contemporary RND. RND is indicated in cases of bulky nodal disease with extensive soft tissue involvement due to extracapsular spread.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r7">[7]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Modified radical neck dissection type I: This procedure involves the removal of lymph nodes from levels I to V, along with the ipsilateral sternocleidomastoid muscle and internal jugular vein, with preservation of the spinal accessory nerve. Modified RND type I is indicated in cases of bulky nodal disease with extracapsular spread involving the sternocleidomastoid muscle and internal jugular vein, when the spinal accessory nerve is not involved by disease.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Modified&#x000a0;radical neck dissection type II: This procedure involves the removal of lymph nodes from levels I to V along with the ipsilateral sternocleidomastoid muscle, with preservation of the internal jugular vein and spinal accessory nerve. Modified RND type II is indicated&#x000a0;for&#x000a0;bulky nodal disease involving the sternocleidomastoid muscle, with sparing of the internal jugular vein and spinal accessory nerve.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Modified&#x000a0;radical neck dissection type III: This procedure involves the removal of lymph nodes from levels I to V, with preservation of the sternocleidomastoid muscle, internal jugular vein, and spinal accessory nerve. Modified RND type III is&#x000a0;indicated for metastatic disease with limited extracapsular spread, in which these structures can be safely preserved and dissected free of tumor involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r9">[9]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Indications for each procedure are summarized below.</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Supraomohyoid neck dissection: This procedure involves the removal of lymph nodes from levels I to III, with preservation of the internal jugular vein, sternocleidomastoid muscle, and spinal accessory nerve. Supraomohyoid neck dissection is indicated for the clinically N0 neck in patients with primary squamous cell carcinoma or malignant melanoma when the primary site is anterior to the ear or located in the lower eyelid. Parotidectomy should be considered for lesions involving the face, forehead, or anterior scalp.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Selective neck dissection: This procedure involves the removal of lymph nodes from levels Ib to IV, with preservation of the internal jugular vein, sternocleidomastoid muscle, and spinal accessory nerve. The procedure is indicated for the clinically N0 neck in patients with squamous cell carcinoma of the lateral tongue, oral cavity, or anterior floor of the mouth, as well as for selected cases of N1 disease at these primary sites.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r10">[10]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Lateral neck dissection (a form of selective neck dissection): This procedure involves the removal of lymph nodes from levels II to IV, with preservation of the internal jugular vein, sternocleidomastoid muscle, and spinal accessory nerve.&#x000a0;Indications for this procedure include N-stage neck disease without significant extracapsular spread with squamous cell carcinoma of the larynx or hypopharynx, as well as for differentiated thyroid carcinoma and melanoma with a positive sentinel lymph node draining to these nodal basins. Selected primary parotid malignancies may also warrant this procedure.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Postero-lateral neck dissection: This procedure involves removal of lymph nodes from levels II&#x000a0; to V, as well as the suboccipital and retroauricular nodal basins, with preservation of the internal jugular vein, sternocleidomastoid muscle, and spinal accessory nerve. This procedure is indicated for node-positive cutaneous melanoma with high-risk features, or for melanoma with a positive sentinel lymph node when the primary lesion is located posterior to the ear.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Modified radical neck dissection: As described above, this procedure is indicated for thyroid cancer and upper aerodigestive tract carcinomas with positive lateral neck lymph nodes and limited extracapsular spread.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r11">[11]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s5" sec-type="Contraindications">
        <title>Contraindications</title>
        <p>Absolute contraindications to neck dissection are limited to conditions that render a patient unfit for general anesthesia or surgical resection, with one exception, which is unresectable disease (see below).</p>
        <p>Relative contraindications include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe cardiopulmonary disease (including chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) with poor functional status</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Preoperative imaging showing deep tumor infiltration in the prevertebral space, scalene muscles, levator scapulae muscle, phrenic nerve, and the brachial plexus</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uncontrollable primary tumor&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of distant metastatic disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Tumor encasement of the carotid artery
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>NOTE: Many authors&#x000a0;consider&#x000a0;carotid artery encasement to represent&#x000a0;"unresectable disease," as resection with grafting does not confer a survival or local control advantage, even when technically feasible.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r14">[14]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Absolute contraindications include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Fixed neck mass involving the deep neck muscles, prevertebral fascia, and/or skull base involvement (unresectable disease).</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s6" sec-type="Equipment">
        <title>Equipment</title>
        <p>A standard surgical instrument set (or tray) for head and neck cancer procedures should include all necessary instruments. Additional specialized instruments may be used at the surgeon's discretion and can include McCabe nerve dissectors, nerve hooks, harmonic scalpels, 0.9-mm forceps, and Munion right-angle clamps.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s7" sec-type="Personnel">
        <title>Personnel</title>
        <p>The surgical team comprises an operating surgeon, an assistant surgeon (resident or registrar, another surgeon, or a certified first assistant), a scrub nurse, a circulating nurse, and an anesthetist.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s8" sec-type="Preparation">
        <title>Preparation</title>
        <p>No specific preoperative preparation is required for patients undergoing neck dissection beyond careful planning of the incision, particularly when resection of the primary tumor is being performed concurrently. Incision planning should also account for any anticipated reconstructive procedures needed to repair the surgical defect following the excision of the primary tumor.</p>
        <p>General endotracheal anesthesia is required for performing a neck dissection, and the use of paralytic&#x000a0;agents should be coordinated between the surgeon and the anesthetist.&#x000a0;The patient is positioned supine with the head elevated to approximately 30 degrees. The neck is extended with a shoulder roll and rotated to the contralateral&#x000a0;side.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r15">[15]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s9" sec-type="Technique or Treatment">
        <title>Technique or Treatment</title>
        <p>
<bold>Incision</bold>
</p>
        <p>Numerous incision types have been described historically, including the Latyshevsky and Freund, Mac Fee, Crile, Kocher, Schobinger, and hockey-stick incisions. In contemporary head and neck surgery, the procedure is typically performed through a single incision placed along a natural neck skin crease at the mid-neck level. Trifurcate incisions, once commonly used, may still be considered in cases of bulky posterior neck disease; however, care must be taken to avoid positioning the trifurcation over the carotid artery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Raising Skin Flaps</bold>
</p>
        <p>The skin incision is deepened through the subcutaneous tissue and then through the platysma muscle. The posterior skin flap is then raised in the subplatysmal plane by applying traction to the flap with skin hooks and countertraction of the deeper soft tissues. The flap is elevated up to the anterior border of the trapezius muscle (see <bold>Image.&#x000a0;</bold>Raised Subplatysmal&#x000a0;Skin Flaps).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Identification of the Marginal Mandibular Branch of the Facial Nerve</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Careful identification&#x000a0;is crucial. Some surgeons advocate avoiding paralytics at this point in the operation. Posteriorly, the greater auricular nerve and the external jugular vein overlying the sternocleidomastoid muscle come&#x000a0;into view as the elevation of the flap continues. The greater auricular nerve and the external jugular vein should be identified&#x000a0;carefully and preserved.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>We advise the use of the scalpel&#x000a0;or scissors to dissect around&#x000a0;the mandibular branch rather than&#x000a0;electrocautery, as it may cause temporary&#x000a0;damage to the nerve. This nerve lies just anterior to the submandibular fascia and superficial to the posterior facial vein. The vein is ligated, and its upper stump retracted cephalad, protecting the marginal branch of the facial nerve (the Hayes Martin maneuver).</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Alternatively, the facial vein and marginal mandibular nerves can be skeletonized and preserved individually. This can greatly increase the available vessel length for microvascular reconstruction and should be discussed between the ablative and reconstructive surgeons preoperatively if it is oncologically sound to perform this dissection. The peri-facial nodes can be dissected out at this point if indicated oncologically.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Superior Dissection</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The fibrous fatty tissue of the submental triangle is dissected off the anterior bellies of the digastric and the mylohyoid muscles. The fascia is then dissected off the anterior belly of the digastric muscle, and the specimen is retracted posteriorly, removing the fibrous fatty tissue containing lymph nodes that are lateral to the mylohyoid muscle. When the dissection reaches the posterior border of the mylohyoid muscle, this is retracted anteriorly, exposing the lingual nerve and the submandibular gland duct.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Finally, the facial artery is ligated as it crosses forward, under the posterior belly of the digastric muscle. Alternatively, the facial artery and vein can be dissected through the substance of the submandibular gland to increase the length of these vessels available for microvascular anastomosis or to facilitate a submental flap.&#x000a0;The tail of the parotid gland is elevated off the sternocleidomastoid muscle and deep structures, and the sternocleidomastoid muscle is then incised close to its insertion in the mastoid process. The fibro-fatty tissue&#x000a0;medial&#x000a0;to the muscle is incised, exposing the splenius capitis and the levator scapulae muscles. Otherwise, incising the fascia below the digastric muscle and with gentle inferior traction of the specimen allows identification of the hypoglossal nerve, the upper end of the internal jugular vein, and the spinal accessory nerve. At this point in the dissection, the internal jugular vein and the spinal accessory nerve are identified and preserved, reflecting the lymph node-bearing tissue inferiorly with the surgical specimen.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Inferior Dissection</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The dissection is continued posteriorly and inferiorly along the anterior border of the trapezius muscle.&#x000a0;The spinal accessory nerve and the transverse cervical vessels are divided as they cross the anterior border of the trapezius muscle.&#x000a0;During this step of the operation, it is important to preserve the branches of the cervical plexus that innervate the levator scapulae muscle, unless the extent of the disease in the neck precludes it.&#x000a0;The sternocleidomastoid muscle and the superficial layer of the deep cervical fascia are incised&#x000a0;above the superior border of the clavicle. The omohyoid muscle is divided, and the external jugular vein can either be preserved or divided, depending upon the vascular needs of the reconstructive surgeon. The fibro-fatty tissue in this region is then gently pushed in an upward direction, exposing the brachial plexus, the scalenus anterior muscle, and the phrenic nerve. Posteriorly, the&#x000a0;dissection is continued to join the previous dissection along the anterior border of the trapezius.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Medial Dissection</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>The&#x000a0;dissection&#x000a0;is continued&#x000a0;medially&#x000a0;to expose the vagus nerve, the common carotid artery, and the internal jugular vein. Inferiorly, the phrenic nerve is&#x000a0;identified&#x000a0;and protected by not violating the deep fascia of the floor of the neck overlying the brachial plexus and muscles. The surgeon must remain very vigilant on the left side to identify the thoracic duct, which arches downward and forward from behind the common carotid to open into the internal jugular vein, the subclavian vein, or the angle formed by the junction of these two vessels. The duct is located anterior or superficial to the anterior scalene muscle and the phrenic nerve.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>To prevent a chyle leak, the surgeon must also remember that the thoracic duct may have multiple connections at its upper end and that, at the base of the neck, it usually receives a jugular, a subclavian, and perhaps other minor lymphatic trunks, which must be ligated or clipped individually.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The internal jugular vein can almost always be spared with meticulous technique, but if resection is necessary, it is important to obtain proximal and distal circumferential control of the vessel with vessel loops before cross-clamping and dividing it. If the tumor mass is located low in the jugulodigastric region or the midjugular&#x000a0;region, the internal jugular vein is first ligated and divided superiorly.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The dissection then continues in an inferior direction, separating the specimen from the vagus nerve, the carotid artery, and the superior thyroid vessels.&#x000a0;If, instead,&#x000a0;the disease is located high in the jugulodigastric region, the internal jugular vein is divided inferiorly, and the dissection continues superiorly along the common carotid artery; this is especially useful when the tumor is extensive and may require removal of the external carotid artery or the hypoglossal nerve. Mobilization of the surgical specimen from below allows easier dissection from the internal carotid artery and, if possible, the external carotid and the hypoglossal nerve.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Closure</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Incision closure is in 3 layers: the first layer approximates the platysma anteriorly and the subcutaneous tissue laterally, and the second layer approximates the subcuticular layer. The skin can then be closed with sutures, staples, or dermal glue. One or 2 suction drains should remain in place, placed in the most dependent areas (the lateral gutter and inferior aspect near the thoracic inlet). The drain(s) should not rest immediately over the carotid artery or in the area of&#x000a0;the thoracic duct, or atop any named nerve.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications may include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Air leak</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Postoperative bleeding</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Chylous fistula:&#x000a0;The reported incidence ranges from 1% to 8% and is more common following neck dissections performed for thyroid malignancy or other procedures in which the central neck compartments are meticulously dissected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r18">[18]</xref> Management depends on the time of fistula onset, the volume of chyle drainage over 24 hours, and the presence or absence of chyle accumulation under the skin flaps. When the daily chyle output exceeds 600 mL or remains between 200 and 300 mL for 3 days, especially when the chyle fistula becomes apparent immediately after surgery, conservative closed-wound management is unlikely to be successful; these findings indicate the need for surgical exploration.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Synchronous bilateral RNDs: Ligation of both internal jugular veins can result in the development of facial edema, cerebral edema, or both, as well as blindness and hypoxia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r19">[19]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Carotid artery rupture:&#x000a0;The most feared and often lethal complication after neck surgery is exposure and subsequent rupture of the carotid artery. Therefore, every effort should be made to prevent this outcome. With appropriate incision design, carotid exposure is uncommon in the absence of a salivary fistula. If the carotid artery becomes exposed, it&#x000a0;should be covered promptly with well-vascularized tissue.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sequelae:&#x000a0;The most notable sequelae observed in patients who have undergone an&#x000a0;RND are&#x000a0;related to the removal of the spinal accessory nerve. The resulting denervation of the trapezius muscle destabilizes the scapula, with progressive flaring at the vertebral border, drooping, and lateral and anterior rotation. Loss of trapezius function decreases the patient&#x02019;s ability to abduct the shoulder above 90 degrees. These physical changes result in a characteristic syndrome of pain, weakness, and deformity of the shoulder girdle commonly associated with RND. Careful performance of a modified RND significantly reduces the severity, likelihood, and duration of these complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r21">[21]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s11" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>Currently, head and neck surgeons throughout the world use a variety of cervical lymph node dissection techniques for the surgical treatment of the neck in patients with cancer of the head and neck region. In contrast to the gradual technical refinements in neck dissection, its role in managing regional disease across the entire gamut of histologies&#x02014;from squamous cell carcinoma to cutaneous melanoma and papillary thyroid carcinoma&#x02014;continues to evolve rapidly.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r22">[22]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Strategies to improve the quality of head and neck cancer care are well established.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-22593.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;Optimal outcomes depend on coordinated interprofessional care. Nurses play a central role in coordinating care, managing documentation, monitoring patients, and promptly reporting complications to the clinical team. The clinical team, in collaboration with nursing staff, must ensure adherence to protocols that prevent infection, dehydration, and inadequate nutritional intake.</p>
        <p>Interprofessional cooperation will help ensure patients achieve the best outcomes. Modern care of the cancer patient is, by definition, multi-disciplinary. Involving speech pathology, dental, pain, auditory, and nutrition providers is a must. Tumor Boards, where all subspecialties and allied health practitioners meet to discuss and plan treatment for new cancer diagnoses, are now standard practice and an essential component of high-quality care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-22593.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
